
  First, the project state, because it changes the shape of this substantially:

  The repo is one commit, one file — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m with an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. There is no task model, no users, no persistence,
  no server, no build tooling. So "notify users when tasks they care about change" currently rests on four things that
  don't exist yet: tasks, users, a notion of "care about", and a change event to hang notifications on.

  That's a decomposition problem before it's a design problem. The fork:

  - [1mNotifications only, against a defined contract[0m — we spec the notification subsystem alone and write down the
    task/user/event interface it assumes, so it can be dropped onto a real app later. Buys a focused spec; costs a
    design validated against an assumption instead of real code. Cheap to change later only if the assumed contract
    turns out close to reality.
  - [1mTasks first, notifications second[0m — we spec and build the task model + change events now, then notifications as the
    follow-on sub-project. Buys a design grounded in code that actually exists; costs one extra cycle before you see any
    notification. Hard to skip later — notifications need this either way.
  - [1mOne combined spec[0m — tasks and notifications designed together in a single pass. Buys coherence; costs a large spec
    covering two subsystems, which is where scope tends to run away.

  My recommendation is [1mtasks first[0m. Notifications are entirely defined by the event stream they consume, and right now
  that stream is hypothetical — anything we spec about delivery, dedup, or "care about" is guesswork until there's a
  real change event to observe. The task layer is also the smaller, more certain piece.
